   "date": "2017-01-18T17:15:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: Factorial Nested ANOVA?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thank you for your feedback. </div><div><br></div><div>I made a new factor PopulationSex:</div><div><br></div><div>PopulationSex &lt;- interaction(Population, Sex) </div><div><br></div><div>and perform analysis:</div><div><br></div><div>myresults &lt;- procD.lm(Size ~ PopulationSex/Tree, iter = 1000, RRPP = TRUE)</div><div>nasted.update (myresults)</div><div><br></div><div>but I don&#39;t understand how to inetrpret the report from nested design:</div><div><br></div><div>Type I (Sequential) Sums of Squares and Cross-products</div><div>Randomized Residual Permutation Procedure Used</div><div>1001 Permutations</div><div><br></div><div>                    Df      SS     MS     Rsq      F      Z   Pr(&gt;F)    </div><div>PopulationSex        5  3380.1 676.03 0.26117 38.567 17.216 0.000999 ***</div><div>PopulationSex:Tree   6  1534.0 255.66 0.11853 14.585 10.479 0.000999 ***</div><div>Residuals          458  8028.1  17.53                                   </div><div>Total              469 12942.3                                          </div><div>---</div><div>Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1</div><div><br></div><div>What I would like to know is whether the interaction Population * Sex is significant when I nest specimens in Trees. Specimens (leaves) from the same tree are not independent observations and represent to a certain degree pseudoreplicates. </div><div><br></div><div>Maybe I should generate mean for every Tree first and than perform analyses? </div><div><br></div><div>Best regards,</div><div>Milena</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "-_--zejPAQAJ",
   "from": "Mike Collyer",
   "author": "aab55242ff4da6c6",
   "date": "2017-01-18T18:48:09Z",
   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] Factorial Nested ANOVA?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div>Milena,</div><div><br></div><div>What your analysis tells you is that the PopulationSex effect is significant with reference to the PopulationSex:Tree effect.  Normally, the F value is found as MS PopulationSex / MS Residuals (notice that the MS for residuals is 17.53, which is a lot smaller than MS for PopulationSex:Tree).  This would be a huge value and potentially pseudoreplication, as your observational units should be trees.  The nested update changes the F value to be MS PopulationSex / MS PopulationSex:Tree, which in this case is still highly significant.  Meaning = The variation among population x sex groups is large relative to the variation among trees within groups.</div><div><br></div><div>The table you want is not something nested.update is currently sophisticated enough to do.  However, if PopulationSex is significant, then it means the combination of Population, Sex, and their interaction are significant.  </div><div><br></div><div>Finding tree means and using a simpler analysis is also something else you can do if you have balanced data (equal number of trees per group).</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers!</div><div>Mike</div><div><br></div><br><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Jan 18, 2017, at 12:15 PM, Milena Stefanovic &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">milen...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div>"
